You are debugging a production issue where a high-throughput microservice migrated to Java 21 Virtual Threads (`Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()`). Under heavy load, the application experiences severe latency spikes and carrier thread exhaustion. You inspect the codebase and find the following bug in a core service:

java}
public class UserProfileService {
    private final Map<String, Profile> localCache = new HashMap<>();

    public synchronized Profile getProfile(String userId) {
        if (!localCache.containsKey(userId)) {
            // Simulating a blocking network I/O call to remote DB
            Profile fetched = databaseClient.fetchProfile(userId);
            localCache.put(userId, fetched);
        }
        return localCache.get(userId);
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where an e-commerce backend uses Spring Boot and `@Transactional` to manage order creation. To improve responsiveness, a developer added asynchronous payment processing using `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync()` inside the transactional method:

java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired private OrderRepository orderRepository;
    @Autowired private PaymentClient paymentClient;

    @Transactional
    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        orderRepository.save(order); // Persisted in local DB transaction context

        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
            paymentClient.charge(order); // Might throw PaymentException
            return null;
        });
    }
}


When `paymentClient.charge(order)` throws a runtime `PaymentException`, the order record created by `orderRepository.save(order)` is still committed to the database instead of rolling back.

You are debugging a production issue where an API Gateway aggregates microservice responses using `CompletableFuture.allOf()`. Under heavy traffic spikes where downstream microservices experience sporadic elevated latency, the application crashes with `java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space`. Profiling reveals millions of uncollected `CompletableFuture$Completion` objects linked together in memory.

java
public CompletableFuture<List<Response>> fetchAll(List<Supplier<CompletableFuture<Response>>> tasks) {
    List<CompletableFuture<Response>> futures = tasks.stream()
        .map(Supplier::get)
        .collect(Collectors.toList());

    return CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0]))
        .thenApply(v -> futures.stream()
            .map(CompletableFuture::join)
            .collect(Collectors.toList()));
}
